LEE BOONSTRA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:07.870
My name is Lee Boonstra.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.940
And I'm a developer
advocate for Google Cloud.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.280
And I'm focusing on
conversational AI.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.250
I'm also the author of the
Apress book "The Definitive

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:19.000
Guide to Conversational AI with
Dialogflow and Google Cloud. "

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.230
You can find me on
Twitter at the @ladysign.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.660
And today I'm going to present
to you the workshop on how

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.840
you can integrate Dialogflow
in your Flutter applications.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.210
For this, we of course,
will need Flutter.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.890
And Flutter is a
development suite

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.860
for building UIs.
In the past, you

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.790
could use this framework
for building mobile UIs.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.610
But lately, you can build
any type of application

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.760
with Flutter, whether it's
also for desktop or for web.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.080
And people choose
Flutter because you

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.730
can very quickly
develop an application,

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.670
and it runs cross-platform.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:59.950
And you can make use of all
these nice UI components.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.940
Now Dialogflow, that is a
development suite for building

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.880
conversational UIs.
So that has everything

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.490
to do with chatbots and
voicebots like speech to text,

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.375
text to speech.

00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:12.000
And then it's powered
of Google Cloud--

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.510
are powered by Google Cloud.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.850
It makes use of
machine learning.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.980
For example, we do
intent detection,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.310
which is kind of like
intent classification.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.380
The way how it
works is Dialogflow

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.291
that runs in the cloud--

00:01:24.291 --> 00:01:26.130
you log in on the interface.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.950
And then as a developer
or a designer,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.370
I feed it training
phrases as examples.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:37.260
And Dialogflow makes use of
an NLU part, natural language

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.510
understanding, so
your end users that

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.910
are typing to your
checkbox or they're

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:46.200
speaking to your checkbox,
it will map whatever they say

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.500
or type to whatever
the training phrases

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.180
that you used in Dialogflow.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.810
And based on that, it will
return you a response.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.650
Now Dialogflow comes with
lots of integrations.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.240
For example, you
can integrate it

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.770
in Twitter or Facebook or web.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.750
But if you want to build
a mobile application,

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.430
yeah, you have to
do this custom.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.990
And that's what we're
going to do today.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:14.610
Now with Dialogflow, you can
create all types of chatbots.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.810
Obviously, the web chatbots
with a textual interface.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.170
And these are like little
popups or iFrames on websites.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.570
But you can also build
voice assistance apps

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.870
with Dialogflow such as
for the Google Assistant,

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.050
or Cobalts where you have
chatbots in telephony contact

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.210
centers.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:37.350
And the last category, these
are the custom voice AI bots.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.410
Maybe you have custom
hardware or IoT devices.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.110
And you want to
integrate your voicebot,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.330
your voice AI on top of that.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.910
Now that is exactly what we
will do today, although we

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.890
don't use custom hardware.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.330
We will use a mobile phone.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:52.950
And in our case, Androids.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:53.680
But it will work.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.950
This workshop will also
run on iOS devices.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.547
Just follow along the steps.

00:02:58.547 --> 00:03:00.130
Now maybe you're
wondering like, well,

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.120
why would you create
your own voice AI?

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.650
Why shouldn't you use
the Google Assistant?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.320
I actually get often
a lot of people

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.700
asking me like, hey, Lee.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.480
How can I integrate the
Google Assistant in my app?

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.980
And let's say you're
a bank, and you

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.280
want to integrate a voice
in your banking app.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.890
And people always ask
me how can I integrate

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.240
the Google Assistant in my app?

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.932
And then I always
ask my customers but,

00:03:24.932 --> 00:03:26.640
like, well, is that
really what you want?

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.090
Because they're-- I mean, the
Google Assistant is a great

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:30.840
ecosystem.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:31.470
And it's great.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.870
But it's also
publicly available.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.830
It runs across every
assistive power device.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:42.760
And you can invoke those actions
by saying the wake words-- so

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.180
"hey G" and "talk to my app."

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.590
And with that, you get
the full ecosystem.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.850
So like, instead of only asking
questions about my banking app,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:54.495
I could also ask the recipes
or the weather information.

00:03:54.495 --> 00:03:56.370
And it might be an
overkill if the only thing

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.480
that you want to do is having
a voice AI in your custom

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.940
mobile app.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.910
So this is exactly
why we're going

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.680
to build our own voice AI.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.570
And we can do this
with Dialogflow.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:12.280
And Flutter will use that
for the mobile app interface.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:14.280
Now there are typically
two approaches

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.350
on how you can
integrate Dialogflow

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.690
in your mobile Flutter app.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.550
The first approach,
which is the approach

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:24.180
that we will use today,
that is by using a Flutter

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.140
package or a Dart package.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.900
And there are some cons and
pros on both approaches.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.060
For the Flutter
package approach,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.530
and that means it's very fast.

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.570
You just include it in
your [INAUDIBLE] pack.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.270
And now you start writing
the code, and it works.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.602
You don't need the back-end.

00:04:40.602 --> 00:04:42.060
You don't need to
pay for a server.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.410
It just works.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.020
But there is an
important part that you

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.450
need to be very careful.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:52.740
As soon as you're going to
build production applications,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.530
you need to be sure
like, what or how

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.180
do you secure your application?

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.880
What do you do with
your service accounts?

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.080
Now when you are building
applications in Google Cloud,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.050
you always make use of an
API key or service account.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:08.130
It is a JSON file.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.730
And a JSON file contains the
project ID of your Google Cloud

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.400
project and also the API keys.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.880
And you give it access
restrictions or access rights

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.830
in order for the
service account to make

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.415
use of all these Google
Cloud technologies.

00:05:22.415 --> 00:05:23.790
And now you could
say, of course,

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.340
like well, I only
assign Dialogflow

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:27.430
to my service account.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.820
So there's not a big deal
if somebody gets access

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.530
to the service account.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.150
Because let's face
it, hackers could

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.480
decompiled your
mobile application

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.828
and then take the service
account out of it.

00:05:38.828 --> 00:05:41.370
And you could think like, well,
it's not a big deal because I

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.710
only give access to Dialogflow.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.690
So nobody can mine
bitcoins on it.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:46.680
Yeah, that's true.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.260
But the fact is with
Dialogflow, we-- or you

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.750
could enable logging
in Dialogflow.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:55.950
And therefore, yeah, you could
collect PII data all the checks

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.040
that are coming in-- yeah,
that's access to your Google

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.090
Cloud account.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.790
If somebody bad has that
service account key,

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:05.890
yeah, then they can get
access to that data.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:08.160
So that's maybe embarrassing
for a production app.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.800
If you do want to go
with this approach--

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.900
because it's
definitely possible--

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.660
but you probably want to do some
additional task on top of it.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.330
Either you want to do
something with rotating tokens.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:22.020
And I'm sure there are Flutter
packages available for that.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.270
Or you maybe run
parts on the back-end,

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.760
or maybe everything
on the back-end.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.430
The nice thing is, since
we're using a package, which

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.760
is Dart code, you can run it
in Cloud Run if you want to.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.600
But you just need to
be careful of this.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.673
For protection apps,
just be careful what

00:06:39.673 --> 00:06:41.340
are you doing with
your service account.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.820
We won't go that deeply today,
but I am pointing it out.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.500
The other approach
that you could use

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.600
is by building the
whole Dialogflow

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.820
part on your server side.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.470
This is great for when you're
building multichannel bots.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.200
So let's say that
you're also creating

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.370
a telephone chatbot
[INAUDIBLE] and it also

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.020
talks to Dialogflow.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.510
And then maybe you want to
build the back-end only once.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:07.050
You can also do this of course,
with Dart and for the code,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.970
or you write it in
some other language.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:09.750
That's fine.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.680
Yeah, but it means,
of course, that you

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.930
need to maintain a server
and also pay for it.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.790
And on top of that,
that's also not as easy.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:20.110
You talk from Flutter to
the back-end with HTTP

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.650
and websockets and
then communicate.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:28.600
Code for an ATP response
could look somewhat like this.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:29.920
But, yeah.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:31.780
And a multichannel
bot-- yeah-- could look

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:32.830
like an architecture like this.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.330
So maybe you have
a Google Assistant

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:35.830
and you have a web chat.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.340
In a Flutter application, they
all talk to the same back-end.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:41.380
It's a really nice architecture.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.510
But again, that takes a lot of--

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:45.700
yeah-- a lot of
knowledge, and when

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.830
you're working in Enterprise,
probably also multiple teams.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.010
| this is exactly
why we're going

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:54.400
with the approach of using a
Dialogflow Flutter package.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.880
I created my own package which
we will use for the workshop

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.060
today.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.450
It's called Dialogflow
underscore GRPC.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.390
And yeah, it will
be a very easy way

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:07.180
to build chatbots and
voicebots-- so speech

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.670
to text bots, which means
that I speak and then it

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:13.490
captures the text and it returns
me an answer on the screen.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:16.470
You can do that
with this package.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:21.090
So before we'll
dive into the steps,

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.680
I'm going to show it
how it will look like.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.090
So here, you can see this is
the app on my Android device.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:30.510
And you will see that there is
like, this little microphone

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:31.890
button that I can press.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.789
And I could ask a question
like, who is using Dialogflow?

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:37.590
And while I'm talking,
you see that it

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.270
starts capturing my voice.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:44.370
And what I press Stop, then
it shows the result on screen.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.470
We'll see it later
also in the emulator.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:50.490
All right.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:56.710
So now I know that this workshop
we'll have a lot of steps.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.560
So it's probably
best if you just

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.280
watch me going through all
the code and explaining to you

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:05.755
all the steps before you're
diving it in yourself.

00:09:05.755 --> 00:09:06.630
I mean, you could do.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:10.470
But I just want to mention it's
a long cold lap where we use

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.660
lots of different technologies.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.330
It's probably best if
you just have a look

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.180
and see how I am
doing these things.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:21.630
The chatbot that we
will build basically

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.480
scrapes FAQs from a website.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.580
And I'm using my own
website in this case

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.360
where I have some Q and A's on
the product Dialogflow flow.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.710
And the chamber will load these
FAQs inside of Dialogflow.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.240
And Dialogflow is then
integrated in Flutter.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.450
So you can ask these
types of questions

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.475
and it will return
you the answer.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:49.615
Now feel free to ask questions,
if you have any questions.

00:09:49.615 --> 00:09:51.240
Or if you have any
comments or feedback

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.950
that you want to
share with me, I

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:55.590
invited today a group
of Google engineers

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.270
that are helping me with
answering the questions.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:00.270
So if you are registered
for the workshop,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:04.200
I think there will be a Q&amp;A
feature in this interface.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.020
Press that button and
start interacting.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.900
And also me, I will
dive into the questions

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.210
afterwards at the end.

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:15.860
Yeah, let's get started!

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:19.540
So I'm expecting but I'm
not of course, not sure

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.900
that you already have a Flutter
environment up and running.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.030
You can have a
Flutter environment

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:26.890
for Mac OS or Windows.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:29.920
You could even run it on
Linux and then Chrome.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.890
But what you will need is
you will basically download

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:34.750
Flutter on your Java pack.

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:37.900
And probably you want to
download Android Studio.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:39.980
And Flutter also comes
with Flutter Doctor.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.200
So when you run Flutter
Doctor on the command line,

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.380
then it will tell you
exactly if you correctly

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:48.250
installed all the tools
and all the emulators.

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:54.580
And if that feedback is all
OK, then you can continue.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:57.400
And what we will do
then is we will start

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:01.060
by creating a Dialogflow agent.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:05.080
And you can do this by going
to the Dialogflow console.

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.980
So you type in the URL
console.Dialogflow.com.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.380
And if it's the very first
time when you log in,

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.480
you log in with
a Google account.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.640
Then you click on
this dropdown bar.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.170
And it's probably empty for you.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.910
As I'm a Dialogflow
developer advocate,

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:21.940
I have many projects here.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.010
But you probably
won't have that many.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:26.405
But you click on
Create New Agent.

00:11:26.405 --> 00:11:28.030
And when you click
on Create New Agent,

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.610
you get this interface
where you specify--

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:33.400
you give it a
name, your chatbot.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.110
You select a time zone.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:37.930
Take the one that's
the closest to you.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:41.350
Please do not switch
the mega agent setting,

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:43.060
because you're not
building a mega agent.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.160
A mega agent is kind
of an orchestrator

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.900
that can talk to many chatbots.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:48.770
But we're not doing that today.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.640
And I would also advise
you to not switch

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:55.960
the global dropdown to switch to
a different region of chatbots,

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.110
because since we're making
use of the FAQ feature,

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:04.610
we are loading FAQs through
the knowledge base in our app.

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:08.710
So that only works
on the global region.

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:13.220
So you create an agent that way.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:19.090
I think, after those steps, you
will click on the cog wheel.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.010
And when you click
on the cog wheel,

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:26.140
you have to enable
the beta features,

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:27.940
because that is the
knowledge base that

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:29.720
will make use of that.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:31.870
Alternatively, you could
go into the Speech tab,

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:35.920
and you could enable the
speech quality settings.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.020
This works best if you are
on a paid Dialogflow plan,

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:43.330
then you can improve
the speech model.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.140
For the workshop,
it's not necessary.

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:48.390
You could leave it
the way as it is.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:49.530
Yeah.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.090
Now the next step
that we will do

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.270
is we will go to the
Google Cloud console

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:57.280
where we will create
a service account.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:01.710
So you go to your
console.cloud.Google.com.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:04.770
And you can start searching
for Dialogflow API.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:08.260
You need to be in the project
that Dialogflow created.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.740
So when you create a Dialogflow
agent, under the hood,

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:13.590
it creates a Google
Cloud project.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.600
So you select that Google
Cloud project in the top.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:21.410
And then in the search bar, you
can search for Dialogflow API.

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:23.360
When you search
for Dialogflow API,

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.280
you should see Manage button.

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:26.240
You click on Manage.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.870
And on the left side there will
be a menu with credentials.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:31.530
You click on Credentials.

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:33.620
And this is the place
where you can start

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:37.200
creating service accounts.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.545
So you create a service account.

00:13:38.545 --> 00:13:39.420
You give it the name.

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:43.390
You can give it a description.

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:44.860
And then you click on Create.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.560
And then the most important
step of this service account

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:49.600
is you need to
assign it some roles.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.390
And we want to give it
Dialogflow access rights.

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:55.630
So you search for the
Dialogflow API admin.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:56.680
You give that.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:58.330
Then you click on Done.

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:02.860
And at that moment, it's
created a service account.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.360
You don't have the key yet.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.480
The key you will get when you
click on the service account

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:08.230
name.

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:11.260
And then you hit
on the Key Step.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:16.000
And from here, you can say
Add Key, Create a New Key.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:17.050
It comes with a popup.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.630
You choose the JSON key
and you click Create.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:22.390
And at that moment,
you store the JSON key

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:24.340
on your hard drive.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:25.600
And later in Flutter.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.385
We will copy this
over to our project.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:32.000
Right.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.220
Now let's start with Flutter.

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:46.070
And we will start by creating an
boilerplate application, which

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:49.310
is like the demo application
that you get when you start

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.040
a fresh new Flutter project.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.690
You can do this in
Android Studio when

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:55.880
you create a new
Flutter application,

00:14:55.880 --> 00:15:00.740
or you could write on the
command line in your terminal

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:05.330
Flutter Create space, and then
the name of your Flutter agent.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.500
So I called it Flutter
Dialogflow agent.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:11.000
And what this does, it generates
the whole folder structure

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:11.900
for me.

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:12.950
That's great.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:15.890
I prefer it to use the
command line personally.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:18.680
That is because I tend to do
a lot of things on the command

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:19.650
line.

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:23.090
But I also have the idea
that it's super fast.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:25.100
So if you don't
have a preference,

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:29.240
I would say maybe choose
the command line as well.

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:33.970
Then you get your
project all generated,

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:36.190
and you can open it in
your favorite editor.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:37.780
I'm using Visual Studio.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.700
But you could also
use Android Studio

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:42.190
that comes out of the box
with that, with Flutter.

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:45.580
And there is an
important setting here,

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:46.550
what we need to do.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:47.950
And we need to do these steps--

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:49.840
the next couple of
steps-- before we

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:53.590
start running our application
and installing our application.

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:57.460
And that is because
the Flutter application

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:00.940
where we will make
use of is making

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:05.320
use of a microphone component.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.360
The component is
called Sound Stream.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:15.280
And that plug-in only
supports the SDK Version 21.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:19.390
So for that, we need to
open the build gradle files.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:22.420
And there are multiple
build gradle files.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:25.600
But you need the one
in the app folder.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.280
So you go into the
App folder and then

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:28.480
you click Build Gradle.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:30.100
And then you scroll down.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:34.150
And here is where you change
the minimum SDK version.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:35.830
I believe by default, it's 16.

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.210
And you need to change it to 21.

00:16:40.038 --> 00:16:41.580
Then there is another
setting that we

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:44.820
need to do-- is we need to give
permissions to our app that

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:46.950
can allow us to talk
over the internet

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:49.860
and that the microphone
is allowed to record.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:52.270
So of course, Dialogflow
is a Cloud solution.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.755
So you always need to be online.

00:16:54.755 --> 00:16:56.880
Therefore, we need to give
the internet permission.

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:59.430
And we need the
recording permission

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:01.560
for in order to
use the microphone.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:05.280
So what we will do is we
will go into the app source

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:10.619
main folder where you can
find the Android manifest.

00:17:10.619 --> 00:17:14.099
And this is the place where
you place the user permissions.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:17.760
You can copy it from the
codelab and paste it right

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:18.990
underneath the opening tab.

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:23.920
And again, please
do this before you

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.230
start installing the
app on your emulator

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:30.040
or in your physical
device, because it might

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:34.320
become difficult otherwise.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:40.890
Now you can see I have my
emulator running because yeah.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:42.960
I'm using an emulator here.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.440
And I could start
typing to the chatbot.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:46.890
I could say hi.

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:50.490
And the chatbot
talks back to me.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:52.660
I could also start
recording my voice.

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:57.790
So I could say who
is using Dialogflow?

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:00.627
And you see that it
was recording my voice.

00:18:00.627 --> 00:18:02.710
Maybe you're wondering
what's that console showing

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:03.340
on the left?

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:06.850
Well, I'm actually logging the
audio stream that is coming in.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:09.400
And as long as these
are not zeros--

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:13.270
so this is a list or an array
with only 8-bit integers--

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:15.310
as long as this
is not zeros, then

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:17.680
I know that there is
audio streaming in.

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:19.990
So at that moment, then
I know that Dialogflow

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:21.290
should work on this as well.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:22.665
So this will be
the end solution,

00:18:22.665 --> 00:18:25.960
what we will eventually go
build speech to text to capture

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:29.230
the audio to written text.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.210
And then we will
match it to a response

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:34.060
from the outflow, which in
my case, is [INAUDIBLE] FAQ.

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:42.230
Before we get there, we need
to add some dependencies

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:44.360
in our application.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:49.070
And like I mentioned, I'm
making use of the sound stream

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:50.510
plug-in in Flutter.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:52.700
This is just an open source
plug-in that I found.

00:18:52.700 --> 00:18:57.638
There are multiple microphone
plug-ins available online.

00:18:57.638 --> 00:18:59.930
You need to plug-in for this
because this is a hardware

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:00.680
feature.

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:03.710
And this one works
on Android and iOS.

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.960
So you can start using it.

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:10.790
You go to your
pops up spec file.

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:14.790
And here, you specify the
dependencies that you're using.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:18.050
So this is the sound stream
one that we're using.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:20.990
We will also make use
of Dialogflow GRPC.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.560
That's the one that I
create that's integrated

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:25.400
with Dialogflow.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:28.370
And maybe you're wondering
what is the RXDart doing?

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:31.280
Well actually, this sound stream
integration, the example's

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:32.270
making use of it.

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:35.700
So that's why it's here.

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:40.200
Now we also need to link it to
our service account, the one

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.820
that we just downloaded
on our hard drive.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:44.880
This is the moment when
you start copying it

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.410
in your project folder.

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:50.010
So what you could
do is you create

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:52.410
a new folder called Assets.

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:55.710
And in the Assets folder,
you drop the JSON key

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:56.460
that you download.

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:58.710
I renamed it to
Credentials with JSON.

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:00.910
And I paste it in
the Assets folder.

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:06.210
And then in my [INAUDIBLE],,
I write down assets--

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:08.200
assets/credentials.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.590
So basically, what I'm
doing is I make a reference.

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:17.060
And if I go too
fast, don't worry.

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:20.630
You will see all these
steps in the code lab.

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:23.480
You can do this later.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.330
I think in [INAUDIBLE] Studio,
when you press Control Save,

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:30.080
it starts downloading all these
dependencies under the hood.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:33.830
If you work from
the command line,

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:36.080
you'll probably want to write
that on the command line

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:37.370
Flutter [INAUDIBLE] get.

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.620
And then it starts downloading
all these libraries

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:40.670
in your project.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:44.360
And you can start
playing around with this.

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:47.030
And again, here's the
big caution warning.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.980
Like, if this is
a production app,

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:52.790
you do want to pay extra
attention on the service

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:55.130
account because
yeah, a hacker could

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:57.410
decompile your
application and then

00:20:57.410 --> 00:21:01.390
get access to it, which
is what we don't want.

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:06.220
Now you can run the application
either on your physical device

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:08.090
or on your virtual device.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:08.920
I showed you both.

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:10.503
In the beginning, I
showed you that it

00:21:10.503 --> 00:21:11.800
was running on the Android.

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:14.500
I also showed you how
it looks into emulator.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:19.135
If you want to run it
on a physical device--

00:21:19.135 --> 00:21:21.010
and actually, if you
don't have a preference,

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:24.550
I probably would go with
the physical device one,

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:28.540
because then you're completely
sure that the audio works

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:32.110
the way how it should
work, while on the emulator

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:35.810
your microphone goes
through your laptop.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:39.520
And I have seen occasions
where the audio got distorted.

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:41.630
And when you have
distorted audio--

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:44.410
I mean, if you would
picture it as a wave,

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:47.920
like a green wave,
like distorted audio,

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:50.770
then would be like very
big and up and downs.

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:53.570
And that means that
also the Dialogflow,

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:55.450
the natural language
understanding--

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:58.540
I mean the speech to
text will probably

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:01.610
have a hard time figuring
out what it means.

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:07.710
So physical device probably
won't have those issues.

00:22:07.710 --> 00:22:08.610
But it might work.

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:11.190
As you've seen on my
laptop, it worked.

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:12.810
Also with my virtual device.

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:15.650
It depends on which
one you create.

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:18.680
On the physical device--
so if you use Android,

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:20.720
the way how you can
debug on your Android

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:23.390
is you will go to
your Android settings,

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:27.410
and then you will tap on your
build number a couple of times.

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:28.880
And on the latest
Android version,

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:30.990
you need to tap it seven times.

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:33.650
And then you get like,
a secret developer menu

00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:36.100
or a developer option screen.

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:37.840
And in this developer
option screen

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:39.430
where you get access to--

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:44.740
that is where you
flip the USB switch.

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:47.470
And once you have the
USB debugging enabled,

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:51.130
then you can just
plug-in your USB cable.

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:55.480
And as soon as you write
Flutter run or you press

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:57.250
the button in your
Android Studio,

00:22:57.250 --> 00:22:59.380
it will recognize
your physical device

00:22:59.380 --> 00:23:02.180
and it starts installing
the APK on your device.

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:04.877
That's amazing.

00:23:04.877 --> 00:23:06.460
If you want to make
use of an emulator

00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:08.260
in your virtual devices,
that's fine too.

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:11.590
You can go into Android
Studio and you can create one.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:16.510
You can pick whatever
operating system and/or Android

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:19.330
version you want and
what type of device,

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:22.430
and you can set it all up.

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:26.150
Now when you do this
for the very first time,

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:29.810
you need to go into
your emulator settings

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:35.120
and make sure that you
enable all the switches.

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:36.710
That allows you to--

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:40.450
yeah-- let the microphone work.

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:42.390
And if you have very
strange problems where

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:44.040
you have that idea
like, well, I don't

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.230
feel that the audio is working.

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:49.830
I don't see zeros in my logs,
but it still doesn't work.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:53.190
Sometimes it might be
wise to record your audio

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:57.870
and play it back in a wave
file through your Flutter code,

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:03.110
because then you can exactly see
how Dialogflow gets the audio.

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:06.580
Now by now what we did is
we created this example app.

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:08.800
It doesn't do anything
with Dialogflow yet.

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:11.470
But that's fine.

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:13.270
Everything is set up
in the way that we

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:18.160
can start using the
right code, which is

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:21.140
what we'll do in the next step.

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:24.280
We will create the
chat interface.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:27.550
And you could copy
it from the codelab

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.260
or you can click on this link.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:32.180
Because I put the code
all in GitHub for you.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:33.080
So that's easy.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:36.310
That means you can just do a
Control-A, Control-C, Control-V

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:38.980
and paste it in your project.

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:45.220
You will create a
chat build Dart file.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:46.410
So that's a new file.

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:48.960
And that's where you
put all the content in.

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:52.890
And this is the place where
we will eventually integrate

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:56.070
our Dialogflow stuff into.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:58.650
We will also create--

00:24:58.650 --> 00:25:00.750
or we already have a main file.

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:03.420
And the main file-- this
is where we link the check

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:05.990
the Dart interface to.

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:10.010
You can also get that from this
link and start copy pasting it.

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:14.720
And what this file does,
it links the chat interface

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:17.220
that you can see that here.

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:20.780
Now what this chat are
doing, it basically

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:22.670
draws out the whole
interface for me.

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:24.740
So in my case, it's
an orange interface

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:29.030
where you see space list view
for all the chat balloons.

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:32.610
You will see a little text
area with two buttons.

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:35.390
One is the Send button, but
it also work on [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:37.490
And you will see the
microphone button

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:42.300
that you can press and hold
to start recording your audio.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.380
That's all for now.

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:47.410
Yes, that's all for now.

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:51.630
I have to speed up time-wise.

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:58.190
But let's quickly go to
the Dialogflow settings,

00:25:58.190 --> 00:25:59.480
what you need to do.

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:04.220
And what I did here is
I created some to-dos.

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:07.340
So I created a to-do.

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:11.240
The first to-do is for loading
the library in your package

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:13.140
or in your chat Dart file.

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:19.075
So you'll load my custom
code, the GRPC file.

00:26:19.075 --> 00:26:20.450
The next step what
you will do is

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:24.580
you will create a
Dialogflow instance.

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:27.730
Then you will integrate
the service account.

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:30.020
And the service account--
now we have the reference.

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:31.640
So this will work.

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:33.790
It links it to the
service account.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:35.470
And once you have
the service account,

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:38.440
you can start to create
a Dialogflow instance.

00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:41.020
And it now knows the project
ID where you're into.

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:45.460
So only your Flutter app can
talk to your Flutter agent.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:48.550
And you can start running
the intent calls on this.

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:54.850
And this is what we're doing
in the HAMIL submit method.

00:26:54.850 --> 00:26:56.320
The HAMIL submit
is that once you

00:26:56.320 --> 00:27:00.430
type in code in your
emulator and you press Enter.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:03.160
Then what it will do, it
will take your written text,

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:09.370
show it on screen, and it will
make the detect intent call.

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:11.380
And what you can see,
this is a future, which

00:27:11.380 --> 00:27:13.330
means it's asynchronous code.

00:27:13.330 --> 00:27:17.830
It loads the text and
it start detecting

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:19.750
and get the answer
from Dialogflow flow,

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:22.480
and stored at in the
fulfillment text.

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:23.950
And from the
fulfillment text, we

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:27.940
will print this on the screen.

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:31.040
Now the second part, that
is the stream method.

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.790
This is the place where we
will create the voicebot,

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:36.010
or in our case,
the speech to text.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:41.170
In the speech to text, what
we will do is we will first

00:27:41.170 --> 00:27:43.690
of all, there is an audio
stream subscription.

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:46.750
This is done for
the microphone part.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:49.660
And what we're doing is as soon
as you press the microphone

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:52.090
button, it starts listening
for the audio stream

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:53.380
and it's logging it.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.390
But it also adds it
to the subscription

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:57.850
and this audio stream.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:00.310
This is what we will
eventually send to Dialogflow,

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:03.670
because that is where
it gets my voice from.

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:05.680
The very important
step here is that you

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:08.320
need to create a
configuration where you

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:12.450
need to assign a sample rate.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:15.000
You need to tell Dialogflow
the sample rate that its using.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:16.830
And that needs to match
to the sample rate

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:18.480
that the microphone is using.

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:21.360
So in our case, the
sound stream package

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:26.060
makes use of 16 PCMI mono-data.

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:30.050
So we need to set the
sample rate to 16,000.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:33.350
And we need to set the encoding
to audio encoding linear 16.

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:36.720
This is very important,
otherwise it won't work.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:40.470
I did an additional step here
I am creating a biased list.

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:47.400
Because my views are making
use of product names and so and

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:53.610
I am not sure if my speech model
will understand these words

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:56.130
or that it will
misunderstood my wording.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:29:00.390
So that is why I'm giving
certain words a boost.

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:02.370
That way I influence
the speech model

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:04.200
to make sure that it
always understands

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:07.617
whatever I'm speaking out.

00:29:07.617 --> 00:29:09.700
Now and then the last thing
what you're doing here

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:12.580
is you do the
streaming detect intent

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:15.910
where you pass in the config
and you pass in the audio stream

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:19.340
that we get from the microphone,
and we start listening in.

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:22.900
So in real time, Dialogflow,
we'll start listening in

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:24.670
and return answers to me.

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:27.020
And if there is
an answer matched,

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:29.650
so if an intent was
matched, then we

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:34.870
are printing it on screen
in an chatbot message.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:38.980
And OK.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:41.770
I have a few more minutes
left and that's great.

00:29:41.770 --> 00:29:45.660
If you've built this,
now you can start

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:49.210
playing around with Dialogflow.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:52.600
And this is also
the moment where

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:54.340
I would say that
you after you're

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:56.080
done with writing
the code you, should

00:29:56.080 --> 00:30:01.250
start testing it in your
emulator or physical device.

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:04.260
The very first time when
you start your application,

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:08.090
you will get a
popup that asks you

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:12.980
if you are having the permission
for using the microphone.

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:16.170
And you need to say yes
while using the app.

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:18.290
And once you do
that, again, make

00:30:18.290 --> 00:30:22.090
sure that you enable the
microphone into emulator here.

00:30:22.090 --> 00:30:24.120
Then it will work.

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:27.240
And then you can start
playing around with this.

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:31.260
And like I said, if you have the
idea that you don't see zeros--

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:34.080
if you don't see zeros, then
there's likely something wrong.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:35.790
Or if you see
zeros, then there's

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:38.370
likely something wrong
with the permissions.

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:40.920
If you don't see zeros,
if you do see numbers,

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:44.430
but Dialogflow is not matching
the audio to whatever you're

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:48.810
saying, make sure that you
have the right sample rate set

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:52.320
and if this matches the
hardware of your device.

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:55.020
And if you're 100% sure
that whatever you're

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:59.790
setting the configuration
is the right setting,

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:01.890
maybe you want to
record your voice

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:06.690
and play that back to be sure
that the audio that comes in

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:09.090
is the right one, or test
it on a physical device,

00:31:09.090 --> 00:31:12.510
because then you're 100% sure.

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:13.900
And then you're good to go.

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:16.920
You can improve the model, as I
said before, in the Speech tab

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:18.210
in Dialogflow.

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:21.585
But you need to be
on the paid version.

00:31:21.585 --> 00:31:23.460
And then you have a much
better speech model.

00:31:23.460 --> 00:31:25.540
But the free version
works fine as well,

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:30.630
especially if you provide the
bias list where you're like,

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:33.900
steering the model to make sure
that, let's say if you would

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:36.660
create an application, you
would say I'm going out

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:39.180
to eat pizza versus
I'm going to Ibiza,

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:41.320
which one is the right one?

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:43.950
Well, it's the one that
you give it the boost.

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:46.740
So that's what
you're doing here.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:50.100
As you've seen, our app
is making use of speech

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:52.830
to text where I use the
microphone to record my voice

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:55.920
and I show the answer back
on the screen in text.

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:57.840
If you're also
curious in like, well,

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:01.530
how would I play back that
audio through the users instead

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:03.000
of showing it on screen?

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:07.080
But yeah, playing it as audio,
well, the good thing to know

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:09.330
is that Dialogflow
can return text

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:12.210
to speech if you enable this
switch in your Dialogflow

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:13.080
interface.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:20.670
And then it returns and base64
stream in your response.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:23.730
You probably need to
do some conversion

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:24.990
to get it in Flutter.

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:29.220
But if you have a look into
the sound stream plug-in,

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:32.765
you do see that they
also have a player.

00:32:32.765 --> 00:32:34.890
So as long as you have a
stream and you attach that

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.265
to the player,
then you can start

00:32:36.265 --> 00:32:37.710
play whatever is in the stream.

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:39.090
So yeah.

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:42.930
You could build this with
text to speech as well.

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:54.140
Now for the last part of
this workshop is the modeling

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:55.400
the Dialogflow agent.

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:57.210
And as I mentioned
in the beginning,

00:32:57.210 --> 00:33:01.340
we were making use of the FAQ.

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:05.510
Here, where I'm loading
FAQ questions in my app,

00:33:05.510 --> 00:33:07.550
the first steps,
what I'm doing here

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:10.970
is I am modifying
the default intents.

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:14.810
So by standard, if you
started flow application,

00:33:14.810 --> 00:33:17.390
you get two intents, the
welcome intent and the fallback

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:18.650
intent--the welcome intent--

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:21.050
that's with the-- that shows
you the welcome message.

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:23.720
And fallback is everything
that can be matched

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:25.310
will go to global fall-back.

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:29.060
And you can start creating more
intents with custom training

00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:32.180
phrases, or what
we will do today

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:35.420
is we will create a knowledge
base, where we link it

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:37.480
and fetch FAQs from a website.

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:42.190
So you click on Create Knowledge
Base, you give it the name.

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:44.800
And now here is the magic.

00:33:44.800 --> 00:33:48.360
You click on Create
The First One.

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:53.520
And from here, you give
it a document name.

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:55.710
You specify the type.

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:58.710
I choose FAQ, because I
have questions and answers

00:33:58.710 --> 00:34:00.780
on my screen.

00:34:00.780 --> 00:34:03.060
You can make it even
more experimental.

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:05.428
If you have a whole
article on the website,

00:34:05.428 --> 00:34:07.470
then you want to import
and let Dialogflow figure

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:08.730
out the questions and answers.

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:10.020
So you could try that as well.

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:12.489
That's with the second option.

00:34:12.489 --> 00:34:14.070
We're choosing FAQs.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:18.310
And this is a website
that is available online.

00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:20.310
It's important that
this website is

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:23.940
available to search
engine bots, because it

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:25.739
uses similar screen
scraping techniques

00:34:25.739 --> 00:34:27.480
as search engines are using.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.060
So you select text
HTML as a MIME type,

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:32.310
and then you specify
the URL that I

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:36.750
have in my set of instructions.

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:38.969
But feel free to
use a different FAQ

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:41.429
if you want to build a
different type of bot.

00:34:41.429 --> 00:34:45.030
I'm loading the FAQs from
my website into Dialogflow.

00:34:45.030 --> 00:34:51.150
And when you click on Save,
it starts scraping that file.

00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:54.300
And it will get all the
questions and answers

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:57.150
in my Dialogflow.

00:34:57.150 --> 00:34:59.640
I can check this check
box to make sure that it

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:01.470
works on automatic reload.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:04.440
So every time when I make
changes on my website, then

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:07.450
a couple of times a day it
will start fetching it again.

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:09.570
So make sure that the
chatbot is up to date

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:12.020
with whatever's on the website.

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:16.990
And if you feel like-- because
the FAQs in Dialogflow,

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:18.760
there is natural
language understanding

00:35:18.760 --> 00:35:19.730
what's behind this.

00:35:19.730 --> 00:35:22.330
So I could mistype this
question and it will still

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:24.230
figure out what the answer is.

00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:27.220
But if I were to ask this
question on a totally different

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:29.620
way, like, instead of
the question in our case

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:32.410
would be like, when should
I use Dialogflow Essentials

00:35:32.410 --> 00:35:34.210
versus Dialogflow CX--

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:37.270
let's say if I would
ask which one is better?

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:38.368
CX or ES?

00:35:38.368 --> 00:35:40.160
That's a different type
of question, right?

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:42.340
But I do want to
return this answer.

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:43.900
What I would do
in that case is I

00:35:43.900 --> 00:35:45.350
would provide training phrases.

00:35:45.350 --> 00:35:46.960
So you promote it to an intent.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:48.880
You say convert to an intent.

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:51.510
It becomes an intent.

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:59.550
And now I can start feeding
it training phrases.

00:35:59.550 --> 00:36:01.320
Which one is better?

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:04.690
And then it will also make a
match on questions like these.

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:06.270
Typically, I would
say give it like,

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:07.860
10 to 15 training phrases.

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:08.970
Then you're good to go.

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:13.200
Then you're training
a very decent model.

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:15.810
Now if you go back to the
Knowledge Base Center,

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:17.370
you see here slider.

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:20.100
There you can specify
like, which one will win?

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:23.280
Will the knowledge base
have a stronger preference

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:25.800
than the default intents or not?

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:30.990
We're going to stick it to the
default, so it's like a 50-50.

00:36:30.990 --> 00:36:32.010
You scroll down.

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:33.300
And this is an important step.

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:35.850
You need to specify
knowledge answer is one.

00:36:35.850 --> 00:36:40.460
So it needs to get the
answer from the question.

00:36:40.460 --> 00:36:43.980
So if you look here, and
like, it cut this block.

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:46.710
But you need to specify
what is the answer that will

00:36:46.710 --> 00:36:48.300
be returned to the chatbot.

00:36:48.300 --> 00:36:50.670
And you could even put
text before it or after it.

00:36:50.670 --> 00:36:54.990
But you specify knowledge
base article one

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:57.780
to make sure that will
be returned on screen.

00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:01.750
Yeah.

00:37:01.750 --> 00:37:02.763
You hit Save.

00:37:02.763 --> 00:37:04.180
And that is the
moment now you can

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:06.220
start testing your application.

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:09.220
And you should see a
similar app like mine. .

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:14.390
And it should work
so, congratulations.

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:16.100
Now if you thought like, wow.

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:17.190
This is amazing.

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:19.850
I would like to play
around more with this,

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:23.540
or maybe you're
interested in Lee,

00:37:23.540 --> 00:37:27.230
how did you actually
built that GRPC plugin?

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:30.020
Well, I explained
this on my weblog.

00:37:30.020 --> 00:37:34.100
So if you go to my weblog,
I'm explaining the steps

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:38.300
on how you can create
your own GRPC package.

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:40.280
Because the GPRC package
is basically auto

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:43.760
generated code
with the SDK specs

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:45.320
that's written in the docs.

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:48.680
So I'm explaining you how you
could do that, just in case

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:51.920
you have other ideas for
Google Cloud components

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:54.420
that you want to run in Flutter.

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:57.840
If you're totally into building
integrations like this,

00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:01.740
definitely stay tuned,
follow me on Twitter

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:07.080
or keep looking into the
comments later on next week

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:10.530
of this video, because we
will introduce a DataFlow CX

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:14.400
competition where developers can
build their own integrations.

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:18.450
Everybody gets a free tee-shirt
and you can win cool prices.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:19.965
So that might be interesting.

00:38:19.965 --> 00:38:21.840
And the other two links
that I'm sharing here

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:24.030
is I created a
white paper on how

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:27.150
you use analytics for chatbots.

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:29.640
And if you're interested
in reading my book,

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:32.750
please look on the
Apress website.

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:36.800
With that, I want to thank you
so much for watching today.

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:40.710
And have a lot of fun during
the rest of the conference.

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:41.210
Thank you.

